{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "toolu_01Ch8MDxSTU8iMp2Xq8hEG9c", "type": "tool_result", "content": "Mon Nov  2 18:48:04 UTC 2026\n\"\"\"Prediction by partial matching on hashed counts, with no gradient descent at all.\n\nAfter Cleary & Witten 1984 (PPM) and Moffat 1990 (PPMC), with the absolute discounting of Kneser & Ney 1995 and the\nrecursive backoff form of Teh 2006 (hierarchical Pitman-Yor language model). The hashing and the fixed memory follow\nMahoney's PAQ family; pruning rare keys before folding follows the same logic as a counting Bloom filter.\n\nThe price list makes this possible. The backward of embedding() is a scatter-add, so the gradient of a sum of table\nlookups is a table of counts, at about 30 ticks per byte per order. One 4e10-tick budget counts the whole training file\nmore than once. Each order keeps a wide table of (context, next byte) keys during training, which is outside the\nparameter cap. The wide table is then pruned (counts below TAU go) and folded into the narrow table that is returned.\n\nforward() reads a count per order per candidate byte and backs off from low order to high:\n\"\"\"Hashed context mixing in the PAQ tradition, trained by gradient descent.\n\nAfter Mahoney's PAQ and lpaq (mixing the predictions of many order-n contexts), with lstm-compress and cmix (Knoll) as\nprior art for neural mixers; the tables are the hashing trick (Weinberger et al. 2009) as used for n-grams by fastText\n(Joulin et al. 2017), and they train with AdaGrad (Duchi et al. 2011). Bengio et al. 2003 is the distant ancestor.\n\nEach order hashes its last n bytes with integer ops into its own table of EMBED-float rows. The rows are concatenated\nand mixed by one squared-ReLU hidden layer into 256 logits. A table row never touched stays zero, so it adds nothing.\n\nWhy the price list favours this: embedding() costs 16 ticks per float backward whatever the table size, so a thin row\nof 8 floats buys a lookup into a huge context for about 140 ticks. A hidden layer of 64 and a head add about 600, cross\nentropy 768. That is about 2600 ticks per byte, against 20000 for the GPT here, so the mixer sees about eight times the\nbytes. Tables take dense updates at about 2 ticks a float, so they update once per TABLE_EVERY batches.\n\nforward() is causal by construction: a position only hashes bytes at or before it. After the softmax it runs a PPM\n\"\"\"The modded-nanogpt speedrun recipe (Keller Jordan et al., github.com/KellerJordan/modded-nanogpt; Muon: Jordan et al.\n2024, \"Muon: An optimizer for hidden layers in neural networks\"), ported to the odometer and pruned by the price list.\n\nMuon with Newton-Schulz orthogonalization on hidden matrices, Adam on the embedding and head. RMSNorm without gains\n(Zhang & Sennrich 2019), rotary positions (Su et al. 2021), QK-norm (Henry et al. 2020), squared ReLU (So et al. 2021),\nuntied head, zero-initialized residual outputs and head, trapezoid schedule. All of those earned their ticks at this\nbudget; three Newton-Schulz steps are the knee, and a window of 48 did at least as well as a deeper or wider model.\n\"\"\"\n\nimport math\n\nimport odometer as od\n 18:48:04 up 3 min,  0 user,  load average: 0.84, 0.90, 0.39", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "eceea530-7843-4b54-956c-e0f4546afa39", "uuid": "b43dba84-3e55-451c-a5fe-f069ce597fb3", "timestamp": "2026-11-02T18:48:04.917Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "Mon Nov  2 18:48:04 UTC 2026\n\"\"\"Prediction by partial matching on hashed counts, with no gradient descent at all.\n\nAfter Cleary & Witten 1984 (PPM) and Moffat 1990 (PPMC), with the absolute discounting of Kneser & Ney 1995 and the\nrecursive backoff form of Teh 2006 (hierarchical Pitman-Yor language model). The hashing and the fixed memory follow\nMahoney's PAQ family; pruning rare keys before folding follows the same logic as a counting Bloom filter.\n\nThe price list makes this possible. The backward of embedding() is a scatter-add, so the gradient of a sum of table\nlookups is a table of counts, at about 30 ticks per byte per order. One 4e10-tick budget counts the whole training file\nmore than once. Each order keeps a wide table of (context, next byte) keys during training, which is outside the\nparameter cap. The wide table is then pruned (counts below TAU go) and folded into the narrow table that is returned.\n\nforward() reads a count per order per candidate byte and backs off from low order to high:\n\"\"\"Hashed context mixing in the PAQ tradition, trained by gradient descent.\n\nAfter Mahoney's PAQ and lpaq (mixing the predictions of many order-n contexts), with lstm-compress and cmix (Knoll) as\nprior art for neural mixers; the tables are the hashing trick (Weinberger et al. 2009) as used for n-grams by fastText\n(Joulin et al. 2017), and they train with AdaGrad (Duchi et al. 2011). Bengio et al. 2003 is the distant ancestor.\n\nEach order hashes its last n bytes with integer ops into its own table of EMBED-float rows. The rows are concatenated\nand mixed by one squared-ReLU hidden layer into 256 logits. A table row never touched stays zero, so it adds nothing.\n\nWhy the price list favours this: embedding() costs 16 ticks per float backward whatever the table size, so a thin row\nof 8 floats buys a lookup into a huge context for about 140 ticks. A hidden layer of 64 and a head add about 600, cross\nentropy 768. That is about 2600 ticks per byte, against 20000 for the GPT here, so the mixer sees about eight times the\nbytes. Tables take dense updates at about 2 ticks a float, so they update once per TABLE_EVERY batches.\n\nforward() is causal by construction: a position only hashes bytes at or before it. After the softmax it runs a PPM\n\"\"\"The modded-nanogpt speedrun recipe (Keller Jordan et al., github.com/KellerJordan/modded-nanogpt; Muon: Jordan et al.\n2024, \"Muon: An optimizer for hidden layers in neural networks\"), ported to the odometer and pruned by the price list.\n\nMuon with Newton-Schulz orthogonalization on hidden matrices, Adam on the embedding and head. RMSNorm without gains\n(Zhang & Sennrich 2019), rotary positions (Su et al. 2021), QK-norm (Henry et al. 2020), squared ReLU (So et al. 2021),\nuntied head, zero-initialized residual outputs and head, trapezoid schedule. All of those earned their ticks at this\nbudget; three Newton-Schulz steps are the knee, and a window of 48 did at least as well as a deeper or wider model.\n\"\"\"\n\nimport math\n\nimport odometer as od\n 18:48:04 up 3 min,  0 user,  load average: 0.84, 0.90, 0.39", "stderr": "", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false}, "tool_result_meta": [{"id": "toolu_01Ch8MDxSTU8iMp2Xq8hEG9c", "permission_decision": {"decision": "accept", "source": "config", "reason_type": "rule"}}]}
