{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CgeeP984QNooQMyxEWDPQ", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I beat the record and submitted one claim: **1.0939 bpb on the dev split** (8 fresh seeds, SD 0.0008, 95% CI ±0.0007), against the record's 1.0994 (SD 0.0016). That's 0.0055 lower, well beyond the 0.002 margin `verify.py` uses for a new record, though I didn't run its formal `--against` test because there's no record log in this copy. The claim file's hash matches the verified run, and it passes the static rules.\n\n## What changed\nThe record's table model and in-window stages are unchanged, except for 6,012 fewer table rows to stay under the parameter cap. The gain comes from how the gate (the small model that weights the in-window stages) is fitted, because it was short of fresh data rather than gradient steps:\n- **Fresh data:** the gate takes one gradient step per batch instead of three, on windows of 256 bytes, so the same ticks cover more data.\n- **MLP removed:** the gate's MLP added nothing measurable, so its ticks go to more data too.\n- **Gate tables:** the previous-byte table matters most, since removing it cost 0.0055 bpb. I added a second table on a hash of the last two bytes, and both now train with AdaGrad.\n\n## How far to trust the number\n- **Selection bias:** I screened 62 variant directories on dev, mostly on seeds 11 and 12. The final seeds were fresh, but the dev windows were the same ones used for screening, so the sealed-split score may come out somewhat higher. The claim says this.\n- **Single-seed evidence:** most of the screening numbers, including the effect sizes in the entry's docstring, come from one or two seeds. Only the 8-seed mean is solid.\n\n## What I left out\n- **Higher gate-table rate (0.2):** it looked about 0.0006 better in screening but scored 1.0941 on 8 fresh seeds, so that gain was noise. I didn't claim it, but the claim's `why` mentions it.\n- **Pretrained gate weights:** these would probably have scored better if packed into a string literal under the literal cap. I didn't try it, because that's training outside the metered budget, which the cap is meant to prevent.\n- **Claims 2 and 3:** I had no other candidate that was verified and meaningfully different, so I didn't use these slots.\n\nThe claim is in `claims/1/` (`train.py`, `claim.json`). The experiment scripts and logs are in `work/`, where `work/c1.json` is the log of the verified run. `work/c1/train.py` was later overwritten by my generator script, so use the copy in `claims/1/`."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 1353, "cache_read_input_tokens": 128642, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 1353}, "output_tokens": 710, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "eceea530-7843-4b54-956c-e0f4546afa39", "uuid": "16b8e26b-ad50-4b13-9442-5270e330e711", "timestamp": "2026-11-02T19:22:13.040Z", "request_id": "req_011CgeeP8rCLQADvEMoWyuMm"}
